## 3. VISUAL DESIGN SYSTEM

### A. Color system

Use a light-mode interface with a predominantly white canvas and International Klein Blue as the distinctive accent.

Establish these design tokens:

- Primary background: #FFFFFF
- Secondary background: #F5F5F7
- Primary text: #1D1D1F
- Secondary text: #6E6E73
- Primary accent — International Klein Blue: #002FA7
- Blue interaction hover: a carefully tested darker blue, such as #00247D
- Subtle borders: #D2D2D7
- Optional neutral surface: #FAFAFA

Use #002FA7 as the initial Klein Blue direction, not as a claim that this is the single canonical pigment formulation. Refine the shade to suit the brand and verify contrast in every context.

Color allocation:

- 80–90% white and neutral surfaces.
- 5–15% typography, imagery, and structural contrast.
- A restrained amount of Klein Blue for high-value visual emphasis.

These percentages are directional, not rigid rules.

Use blue for primary actions, active states, selected options, important data points, small visual signatures, and purposeful interactive highlights.

Do not make every heading blue. Do not apply blue to every icon, border, or background. Do not use blue merely to fill space.

Avoid unnecessary rainbow gradients, excessive glassmorphism, neon glows, colorful shadows, and uncontrolled gradients.

### B. Typography

Use Apple's typographic discipline without copying proprietary assets or claiming access to Apple's custom font files.

Preferred system stack:

-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "SF Pro Display", "SF Pro Text", "Helvetica Neue", sans-serif

Use a high-quality, properly licensed alternative when the preferred system font is unavailable.

Establish a consistent type scale:

- Hero display: approximately 64–96 px on large desktop screens.
- Section headline: approximately 40–64 px.
- Feature headline: approximately 28–40 px.
- Body text: approximately 16–20 px.
- Supporting text: approximately 14–16 px.
- Navigation and labels: approximately 12–14 px.

Adjust these sizes responsively rather than enforcing fixed dimensions.

Typography rules:

- Use large, confident headlines with compact but comfortable line heights.
- Keep hero text blocks relatively narrow.
- Use regular and medium weights for most text.
- Reserve heavy weights for deliberate moments of emphasis.
- Establish clear differences between display, heading, body, caption, and interface text.
- Maintain readable line lengths and sufficient paragraph spacing.
- Use sentence case unless a brand-specific reason justifies another style.
- Avoid excessive uppercase labels, decorative lettering, and gratuitous letter spacing.
- Never sacrifice readability for an oversized headline.

Use fluid typography where appropriate, such as CSS clamp(), with sensible minimums and maximums.

### C. Spacing and layout

Adopt a consistent spacing scale based on multiples of four or eight.

- Large desktop content width: approximately 1200–1440 px.
- Main horizontal padding: approximately 5–8vw on desktop, adjusted to content.
- Section spacing: approximately 96–160 px on desktop.
- Component spacing: approximately 16–40 px.
- Mobile horizontal padding: approximately 20–24 px.
- Use generous negative space around important product visuals and headlines.

Use a consistent responsive grid, disciplined alignment, and clear content boundaries.

Do not force every section into identical layouts. Introduce variation through intentional composition: centered hero sections, split editorial layouts, oversized image sections, horizontal comparison tables, and carefully designed full-width demonstrations.

### D. Surface and component styling

- Prefer flat, clean surfaces.
- Use thin borders only when they clarify grouping or interaction.
- Apply rounded corners consistently and sparingly.
- Avoid making every component a floating card.
- Use subtle shadows only when they establish meaningful elevation.
- Keep buttons, selectors, inputs, and navigation elements visually consistent.
- Make interactive elements recognizable without excessive decoration.

The interface should feel engineered rather than ornamental.

